From 62eaca871a141965692badc33773983e70ba9e6e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nekokabu <nekokabu@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 21:57:22 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 29/32] n64: what a CPU cache line fill costs the RDP depends
 on what the RDP is doing (rdp_timing v60 to v68)

Since patch 0028 a CPU data cache fill in a megabyte the RDP does not use
cost the RDP 9.95 clocks and an instruction cache fill 6.1, whatever the
RDP was drawing. On the console the loss differs from list to list
(rdp_timing v61, the CPU's fills beside 29 game lists, VI off; data fill
/ instruction fill):

  MGC 2011 demo, write-only parts        11.0 / 7.0
  MGC 2011 demo, part 5                  10.4 / 6.3
  MGC 2011 demo, blended part             9.0 / 5.7
  Beetle Adventure Racing, first scene    9.6 / 6.7
  Beetle Adventure Racing, second scene   8.8 / 5.8
  Donkey Kong 64                          8.2 / 5.2

rdp_timing v63 to v68 measured the loss beside lists of one kind of work
each, and beside game lists with one kind of work removed:

  fill rectangles, 33 pixels and wider   12.2 / 11.9   (narrower: 7.1 / 5.6)
  copy rectangles                         6.1 /  4.6
  texture loads                           4.2 /  4.0
  triangle set-up, state commands         4.6 /  4.4
  spans passing the depth test           10 to 12 / 6 to 8, whatever the width
  spans failing the depth test            9.7 down to 3 / 5 down to 2, the wider the less
  blended spans without depth            10 to 11.6 / 6 to 7.6
  write-only spans                       13 to 14 / 9.4 down to 5.8 (65 pixels and wider: 5.5 / 3.6)

(the synthetic triangles of v60 to v64 all had the same depth, so their
wide spans mostly failed the test; v65 drew them far to near.) What share
of the depth-compared spans passes also matters: the same game list costs
8.0 to 10.1 clocks per data fill with every first draw passing and 5.7
to 7.8 with every test failing (v65). 1 / overdraw of the last frame is
used for that share (Donkey Kong 64: 0.57, measured 0.56 in v67).

RDP::timingSpan() and the texture loads now sum each frame's time by
kind (RDP::timingLoss), the rest of the frame's time counts as set-up,
and at Sync_Full the average becomes what the CPU's transfers cost
beside the next frame. The terms of patch 0028 for the place of the
transfer and the VI's factors of patch 0025 are unchanged.

Prediction against the console over the 78 lists and variants of v61 to
v65, nothing fitted: rms 0.73 clocks (data fill) and 0.63 (instruction
fill); with the two constants 1.56 and 0.88. Largest remaining
differences: Beetle Adventure Racing's second scene +0.8 / +0.5 and
Donkey Kong 64 +0.5 / +0.7.

Results in the test harness (series v24 against v23): Donkey Kong 64's
DKTV demo 10 / 378 / 35 frames of delta 2 / 3 / 4 and more (was 7 / 371 /
42; console 9 / 385 to 391 / 25) and +0.8% VI over the demo (was +1.3%);
Beetle Adventure Racing's intro 238 / 321 frames in the two segments (was
236 / 316; console 231 / 325); the MGC 2011 demo's "@" frame +34 VI and
the start of part 4 +11 VI (was +32 / +14). rdp_timing v61 run in the
harness, data fill by scene against the console: MGC -0.2 clocks, BAR's
first scene +0.1, its second scene +1.0, DK64 +0.6 (was -0.4, +0.4, +1.3,
+1.8).

The losses above were read from DPC_CLOCK, which on the console keeps
counting until the test ROM notices the end of the list: up to one chunk
of the CPU load later (about 7k clocks for the data loads, 30k for the
instruction load). DPC_PIPEBUSY stops with the RDP and gives 0.03 to 0.1
clocks less per data fill and 0.2 to 0.5 less per instruction fill
(v61). The values of this patch are the DPC_CLOCK ones.
